{"reply":"In Chapter 5, physical change and identity uncertainty become almost the same problem. When the Caterpillar asks, “Who are _you?_” Alice says she hardly knows who she is because she has been changed several times; she then connects that uncertainty directly to her shifting body, which she describes as confusing because she is repeatedly different sizes.

Her changing size undermines the stable continuity that normally lets her recognize herself: if her body, memory, and abilities keep changing, she cannot confidently say that she is the same Alice. At the same time, her uncertain identity makes each physical change more troubling, because size is no longer just a temporary bodily condition—it becomes evidence that she may be becoming someone else. The Caterpillar’s repeated question therefore exposes the feedback loop: Alice cannot know who she is because she keeps changing, and the changes feel so destabilizing because she no longer knows who “she” is.

