{"reply":"In Chapter 5, physical change and identity uncertainty become almost the same problem. When the Caterpillar asks, “Who are _you?_” Alice says she hardly knows who she is because she has been changed several times; she connects that uncertainty directly to her shifting body, which she describes as confusing because she is repeatedly different sizes.

The passage invites us to read Alice’s size changes as a physical image of her unstable sense of self. Her difficulty remembering things “as [she] used” to and her inability to keep the same size suggest that both memory and the body—ordinary anchors of identity—have become unreliable. In that sense, each change in size intensifies the question of who Alice is, while her uncertainty about herself makes the bodily changes feel more than merely physical. The Caterpillar’s repeated question exposes this reinforcing loop: Alice’s changing body makes identity difficult to hold onto, and her identity crisis gives those bodily changes their deeper significance.

